  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1042 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nMostly cloudy conditions will persist across the Mid-South into\nthe overnight hours as the remnants of Isaias move through the TN\nRiver Valley. Latest radar scans do still show some light rain\nalong the TN River and across northeast MS, which may linger into\nthe early morning hours on Sunday. Otherwise, Isaias will quickly\npull northeastward through the day on Sunday, with clearing\nexpected across much of the Mid-South. A much drier airmass will\nquickly move into the area through the day on Sunday, with\ntemperatures rebounding quickly for the majority of the area.\nOutside of some temperatures near 80 along the TN River where\nsome cloudiness may persist, the vast majority of the area will\nrise into the mid to upper 80s by the afternoon. By Monday, high\npressure will begin to develop to our south, with humidity levels\nbeginning to creep back up by Tuesday. Through the first half of\nthe work week, high temperatures will be in the low 90s, which is\nabout 10 to 15 degrees above normal for the middle of October.\nFor further reference, the current high temperature records for\nMemphis Monday through Wednesday are 92, 92, and 90 respectively.\nOur forecast high temperature each day would either be close to\nor exceed these values. A similar trend can be noted at Jackson,\nJonesboro, and Tupelo as things currently stand. So, potential\nrecord breaking heat has found its way back to the Mid-South\nunfortunately.\n\nBy Wednesday, a weak shortwave will pass around the\naforementioned high pressure system, with the potential to bring\nsome precipitation briefly back to northern portions of the Mid-\nSouth. This could play spoiler to the aforementioned high\ntemperatures. However, given the set-up, confidence is not that\nhigh in seeing any meaningful precipitation for most of the area.\nLocations north of the I-40 corridor could see up to half an inch\nof precipitation at this point. By Thursday into the back half of\nthe week, all eyes will turn to the west as a low pressure system\nmoves off of the Rockies. While the Mid-South will likely remain\nwarm (highs in the upper 80s to low 90s) through Thursday,\nsignificant uncertainty arises Friday into next weekend. Guidance\nis suggesting a fairly potent upper-level system will move across\nthe eastern portion of the U.S., although timing and how far\nsouth it initially reaches will play a significant role in our\nweather pattern next weekend. GEFS solutions are favoring a merge\nbetween the upper-level pattern and a closed low off the Rockies,\nwith a deeper trough passing through the Mid-South bringing a\nsignificant cool down. ENS guidance is a bit more split, with the\ntrough not deepening until further east, bringing less of a cool\ndown. Regardless, guidance is trending towards more seasonable\ntemperatures returning next weekend. Keep those fingers crossed\nif you have been wishing for fall-like weather!",
